{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n93 \n \n \nAppendix M. Summary of the ISS data collection \nThe Flame Design data is all digital and includes both image and non-image data, where that is generally \ntrue for the ACME experiments.  To be clear, there was no collection of soot or other physical samples \nthat were subsequently transported to the ground.  \n \nThe Flame Design image data includes images from the following cameras: \n1. ACME DataCam, used with a clear fused-silica \u2018filter\u2019 and various zoom and focus settings (but \nthose settings were consistent within each individual test) \n2. CFI intensified camera, used at least generally with a CH* filter \n3. High-Bitdepth Multispectral (HiBMs) camera, equipped with a red filter corresponding to the \nopposing CIR illumination package \n \nThe raw DataCam images are in a Halcon Iconic Object (hobj) format as well as a converted Tagged Image \nFile (tif) format.  NASA\u2019s post-processing of the raw images was carried out without each of the following: \na. color balancing (to avoid possible saturation)  \nb. dark frame subtraction \nc. removal of hot spots from dead pixels (e.g., resulting from radiation damage)  \nFurthermore, the conversion employed a standard bilinear demosaicing process which is detrimental \nwhen determining temperature profiles via Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) because it will average pixels \nboth on and off the glowing fibers. \n \nThe images from the CFI and HiBMs cameras are provided in a tif format.  Meanwhile, the analog \noperations (ops) video was recorded on the ground in a Transport Stream (ts) format. \n \nThe key non-imaging data from a test is a comma-delimited file that is described in detail in the Software \nInterface Design Description (ACME-DOC-010 Rev. D). \n \nThe CIR\u2019s Gas Chromatograph Instrumentation Package (GCIP) was used during a limited number of Flame \nDesign test days and the resulting raw data files are in a text (i.e., ASCII) format. \n \nConsult the forthcoming ACME/Flame Design Data Interpretation document to learn more. \n \nThe raw Flame Design data is currently archived at NASA and work is underway to make it available \npublicly through NASA\u2019s Physical Science Informatics System (PSI).", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 93}}